# Sparrow receiver: [[.TargetName]]

A receiver for Sparrow webhook `[[.WebhookID]]`[[if .Consumer]] (consumer `[[.Consumer]]`)[[end]]. It verifies every
delivery's signature, drops redeliveries, routes each event to its handler and
answers fast. Put your logic in the handler stubs marked `TODO: your logic`.

## Run

```sh
[[- range .Setup]]
[[.]]
[[- end]]
[[.Run]]
```

[[if .Ed25519 -]]
Deliveries are verified with the webhook's Ed25519 public key, embedded in the
code (it is public). Set `[[.EnvPublicKey]]` to use another key.
[[- else -]]
Set `[[.EnvSecret]]` to the webhook's signing secret (`whsec_...`) from Sparrow.
Never commit it; `.env.example` lists every variable.
[[- end]]
The receiver listens on `PORT` (default [[.Port]]) and serves:

- `POST [[.WebhookPath]]`: deliveries. Point the webhook's URL here.
- `GET [[.HealthPath]]`: liveness.

## Test

```sh
[[.Test]]
```

The tests replay `testdata/spec.json`: signed requests for each subscription,
including tampered, unsigned, stale, future-dated and duplicate ones. They are
signed with a throwaway [[if .Ed25519]]key pair[[else]]secret[[end]] derived from the webhook ID, never the
webhook's real [[if .Ed25519]]key[[else]]secret[[end]].

With `[[.EnvTestMode]]=1`, the receiver writes one JSON line to stdout
per handler call (`handler`, `subscription_id`, `event_type`, `webhook_id`,
`value`). It is off by default; use it for contract tests, not in production.

